#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A stand-in for the 1Password CLI (`op` 2.x) for Copper's tests.

It answers the subcommands Copper's 1Password backend uses, with op's JSON
shapes, from a JSON state file instead of a 1Password account:

    --version | account list | account add | signin | signout | whoami
    vault list | item list | item get (ID, NAME or `-` on stdin) | item create - | item edit ID

Sign-in works the three ways the real CLI does:

* the 1Password app: with `app_integration` on, `op signin` "asks the app" and
  is approved (`app_approve`, default on) or dismissed; nothing comes back on
  stdout and later commands are authorised by the app session;
* account password: `op signin --raw` reads the password from stdin and prints a
  session token; later commands carry it in OP_SESSION_<user id>; `op account
  add --signin --raw` takes the Secret Key from OP_SECRET_KEY;
* service account: OP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=ops_… (read-only unless the token
  may write to a vault).

Mock-only commands (not in the real CLI):

    op mock show            the state without any secret (passwords as sha256 prefixes)
    op mock expire          every session and app authorisation ends now
    op mock set KEY VALUE   a flag: app_integration, app_approve, latency_ms, app_delay_ms,
                            hang_ms (every command but `mock` sleeps this long first, outside
                            the state lock — an op that hangs), malformed (item list/get
                            answer with broken JSON)
    op mock bulk N          add N generated logins (unicode titles, several URLs, shared hosts)
    op mock reset           back to the seed
    op mock check TITLE USER PASSWORD   does that login hold exactly this? (true/false)

State lives in $OP_MOCK_STATE (default ~/.config/op-mock/state.json); every call
is appended to calls.log beside it — argv and the *names* of OP_* variables,
never their values or stdin — so a test can prove no secret went on argv.
"""
